q('A patient from a Strongyloides-endemic region is about to receive high-dose steroids. What is an important preventive step?', 'Assess for Strongyloides and arrange indicated treatment through a timely risk-based pathway',[
 ('A normal eosinophil count permanently excludes infection','Eosinophilia can be absent.'),('Steroids eradicate chronic Strongyloides','They can precipitate hyperinfection.'),('One routine negative stool examination excludes every chronic infection','Routine stool sensitivity is limited.')],
 'A chronic silent infection can become lethal after immunosuppression', '**Strongyloides autoinfection permits persistence for years.**',['Epidemiological risk remains relevant long after exposure.','Serology and appropriate stool methods have complementary roles.'], '**Coordinate screening or treatment without unsafe delay in urgent care.**',['Ivermectin is the usual preferred therapy when appropriate.','Assess Loa loa exposure where relevant before empiric ivermectin.'],[['Risk','Endemic exposure plus immunosuppression'],['Mechanism','Autoinfection'],['Prevention','Timely risk-based evaluation/treatment']],[SR,SO],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A steroid-treated patient develops pulmonary infiltrates, gastrointestinal symptoms and Gram-negative bacteremia; larvae are found in sputum. What is most concerning?', 'Strongyloides hyperinfection or disseminated strongyloidiasis',[
 ('Uncomplicated intestinal colonization with no systemic risk','Sputum larvae and severe illness indicate a dangerous syndrome.'),('Ascaris infection as proven by every sputum larva','Organism identification and the autoinfection context matter.'),('A normal eosinophil count would exclude the diagnosis','Eosinophilia can be absent in severe disease.')],
 'Accelerated autoinfection can spread larvae and facilitate bacterial invasion', '**Corticosteroid exposure is a major trigger.**',['Enteric bacterial sepsis can accompany larval migration.','Respiratory failure and ileus complicate treatment delivery.'], '**Begin urgent effective therapy and organ support.**',['Reduce immunosuppression when safely feasible.','Treat associated bacteremia and assess absorption of oral drugs.'],[['Trigger','Steroids/immunosuppression'],['Evidence','Larvae beyond the usual intestinal setting'],['Complication','Sepsis and multiorgan disease']],[SR,SO],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What is the treatment principle for Strongyloides hyperinfection?', 'Daily ivermectin-based treatment with follow-up parasite clearance and management of immunosuppression and complications',[
 ('A single routine deworming dose always completes treatment','Severe disease requires an extended clearance-based approach.'),('High-dose steroids as the only pathogen treatment','They can worsen the disease.'),('No further therapy once diarrhea stops despite persisting larvae','Clinical symptom change does not prove clearance.')],
 'Severe autoinfection needs more intensive treatment than an uncomplicated course', '**CDC recommends continued therapy until stool/sputum examinations are negative for the indicated interval.**',['Ileus or malabsorption can require specialist alternative delivery planning.','Associated bacterial infection needs active treatment.'], '**Monitor the whole patient during parasitological clearance.**',['Assess respiratory and nutritional support.','Follow for recurrence when ongoing immune impairment remains.'],[['Drug','Ivermectin'],['Endpoint','Documented parasite clearance'],['Parallel','Immune-risk reduction and complication care']],[SR])
q('A patient with seizures has only calcified parenchymal neurocysticercosis lesions and no viable cysts. Which approach is recommended?', 'Symptomatic seizure management rather than antiparasitic treatment of the calcifications',[
 ('Albendazole is mandatory to kill every calcified lesion','Calcifications do not contain viable parasites requiring that treatment.'),('No antiseizure treatment can ever be needed','Calcified lesions can remain epileptogenic.'),('Routine surgery for every asymptomatic calcification','This is not the standard approach.')],
 'Parasite viability determines the role of antiparasitic drugs', '**Calcified parenchymal NCC is managed symptomatically.**',['Perilesional edema can occur and requires clinical assessment.','MRI may help when symptoms suggest additional lesions or complications.'], '**Treat the seizure disorder and assess individual risk.**',['Do not infer that old calcification makes all new neurological symptoms harmless.','Viable, ventricular and subarachnoid disease have different pathways.'],[['Calcified','No viable parasite target'],['Symptoms','Seizure-directed care'],['Other compartments','Different treatment strategy']],[NC],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Before antiparasitic treatment for neurocysticercosis, which assessment is important?', 'Evaluate for intraocular cysts and clinically important intracranial-pressure or compartment-specific disease',[
 ('Begin treatment blindly because inflammation cannot worsen symptoms','Parasite killing can provoke inflammatory injury.'),('A normal blood eosinophil count excludes ocular disease','It does not.'),('Every lesion, regardless of site or viability, uses the same regimen','Site and stage materially change management.')],
 'Killing a parasite can create a dangerous inflammatory response', '**Funduscopic assessment helps exclude intraocular cysticerci.**',['Hydrocephalus and diffuse edema may need control before antiparasitic therapy.','Anti-inflammatory treatment is selected according to the regimen and disease form.'], '**Plan treatment from imaging, viability and site.**',['Use specialist neurology/infectious-disease input for complex disease.','Do not apply a single parenchymal-lesion rule to ventricular or subarachnoid disease.'],[['Eye','Exclude intraocular cysts'],['Pressure','Assess hydrocephalus/edema'],['Regimen','Stage- and compartment-specific']],[NC])
q('An asymptomatic patient has an uncomplicated inactive hepatic echinococcal cyst on appropriate staging. Which approach fits WHO 2025 best practice?', 'Imaging follow-up with watchful waiting when there is no complication requiring intervention',[
 ('Immediate surgery for every inactive cyst','Unnecessary surgery can cause harm.'),('Unplanned aspiration in the clinic','Intervention requires a stage-specific expert plan.'),('Indefinite high-dose albendazole for every inactive lesion','Inactive disease does not automatically require that exposure.')],
 'Hydatid management is stage-specific rather than uniformly interventional', '**Inactive uncomplicated cysts can be observed.**',['Size, location, symptoms and complications affect the decision.','Active and transitional stages can require different treatments.'], '**Use reliable staging and follow-up.**',['Do not ignore new pain, infection or mass-effect complications.','Review intervention only when its expected benefit outweighs risk.'],[['Inactive uncomplicated','Watch and wait'],['Active/complicated','Different pathway'],['Monitoring','Serial appropriate imaging']],[EC],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Why should a suspected hydatid cyst not undergo casual diagnostic aspiration without planning?', 'Spillage can cause anaphylactic reactions and dissemination, while percutaneous therapy requires appropriate staging and precautions',[
 ('Every percutaneous procedure is absolutely prohibited','PAIR and other techniques have defined roles in selected cysts.'),('Aspirated fluid never contains infective material','That assumption is unsafe.'),('Antibiotics alone remove every procedural risk','They do not address anaphylaxis or parasite spillage.')],
 'A useful intervention can be unsafe outside its validated pathway', '**PAIR is a selected therapeutic technique rather than an unplanned sampling maneuver.**',['Cyst stage and communication with biliary structures matter.','Antiparasitic and procedural precautions require expertise.'], '**Choose observation, drugs, percutaneous treatment or surgery from the actual cyst.**',['Use imaging-defined anatomy.','Ensure appropriate emergency capability when intervention is indicated.'],[['Casual aspiration','Avoid'],['Selected percutaneous therapy','Can be appropriate'],['Decision','Stage, anatomy and expertise']],[EC])
q('Which exposure classically transmits schistosomiasis to humans?', 'Skin contact with freshwater containing cercariae released from infected snails',[
 ('Eating undercooked pork containing cysticerci','That relates to intestinal taeniasis rather than schistosomiasis.'),('A chigger bite','That is relevant to scrub typhus.'),('Inhaling desert fungal spores','That is a different infection route.')],
 'The infective stage penetrates skin during water exposure', '**Freshwater contact can occur during swimming, bathing or work.**',['Acute symptoms may precede detectable egg shedding.','Chronic manifestations depend on species and egg deposition site.'], '**Use exposure timing to choose testing and treatment.**',['Avoid assuming an early negative stool or urine test excludes infection.','Counsel about future freshwater avoidance in endemic settings.'],[['Vector host','Freshwater snail'],['Infective stage','Cercaria'],['Entry','Skin penetration']],[SE])
q('Why is praziquantel treatment in a newly exposed traveler commonly timed at least six to eight weeks after the last freshwater exposure?', 'It is most effective against mature adult schistosomes rather than very early immature stages',[
 ('It has no activity against any adult schistosome','Adults are its key target.'),('Waiting always removes the need to assess acute severe illness','Acute illness still needs prompt evaluation and separate management.'),('The interval proves the infection cannot persist later','Chronic infection can persist without treatment.')],
 'Parasite maturation affects treatment effectiveness', '**Praziquantel is the drug of choice for major Schistosoma species.**',['Recently acquired infection can require later repeat treatment.','Species determines the appropriate dose schedule.'], '**Assess the acute syndrome and plan follow-up.**',['Do not delay care for severe neurological or systemic disease.','Use repeat egg testing when appropriate after a documented infection.'],[['Target','Adult worm'],['Traveler timing','Usually ≥6–8 weeks after exposure'],['Follow-up','Repeat treatment/testing when indicated']],[SH])
q('A patient from a Loa loa-endemic area needs ivermectin for another suspected parasite. Which safety issue is important?', 'High Loa loa microfilarial burden can predispose to severe treatment-associated encephalopathy',[
 ('Loa exposure is irrelevant because ivermectin has no effect on microfilariae','Microfilarial killing is relevant to the risk.'),('A normal eosinophil count quantifies the microfilarial load','It does not.'),('Every exposed patient can receive any regimen without specialist assessment','The burden and clinical context can materially change safety.')],
 'Treating one parasite can uncover risk from a coexisting infection', '**Quantitative microfilarial assessment may be required in an exposed patient.**',['DEC and ivermectin can cause severe neurological events in high-burden loiasis.','The exact strategy requires expert guidance.'], '**Balance treatment urgency and avoid blind dosing.**',['Use the appropriate geographically informed screening pathway.','Arrange specialist management when high burden is possible.'],[['Risk','High Loa microfilaremia'],['Potential harm','Encephalopathy'],['Action','Burden assessment and expert plan']],[LO])
q('After eating undercooked wild-boar meat, a patient develops fever, myalgia, periorbital edema and eosinophilia. Which infection is characteristic?', 'Trichinellosis',[
 ('Giardiasis','It causes a different intestinal syndrome without this muscle-migration pattern.'),('Urinary schistosomiasis','The exposure and clinical features differ.'),('Falciparum malaria as proven by periorbital edema','The meat exposure and eosinophilic myositis point elsewhere.')],
 'Larval migration to muscle creates a characteristic systemic phase', '**Trichinella is acquired from infected undercooked meat.**',['Gastrointestinal symptoms can precede the muscle phase.','Severe cases can involve myocardium or CNS.'], '**Use exposure, serology and selected direct evidence.**',['Early testing can require follow-up.','Assess severity and begin an appropriate treatment plan.'],[['Exposure','Undercooked infected meat'],['Pattern','Myalgia, edema and eosinophilia'],['Complications','Cardiac or neurological involvement']],[TR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which statement about toxocariasis is correct?', 'Larval migration can cause visceral eosinophilic disease or unilateral ocular inflammation, with testing interpreted in that clinical context',[
 ('A routine stool egg examination reliably diagnoses human tissue larval infection','Humans do not usually harbor egg-producing adult intestinal Toxocara.'),('Ocular disease requires marked blood eosinophilia in every case','Systemic findings can be limited in ocular disease.'),('Every positive antibody proves the cause of any unrelated symptom','Exposure antibodies need clinical attribution.')],
 'Humans are accidental hosts for tissue-migrating larvae', '**Visceral and ocular disease have different presentations.**',['Exposure can involve contaminated soil or other relevant sources.','Ocular disease can threaten vision and needs specialist assessment.'], '**Integrate exposure, serology and organ findings.**',['Do not rely on routine stool microscopy for tissue larvae.','Coordinate anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic decisions for the actual syndrome.'],[['Visceral','Multiorgan/eosinophilic syndrome'],['Ocular','Often unilateral'],['Diagnosis','Clinical and exposure context plus appropriate tests']],[TX])

